L2 -> def-additive-function [Definition;2]: Let $\mathbb{R}$ be the complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]], [[def-field]]). A function $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ is **additive** when it satisfies **Cauchy's functional equation**
L3 -> cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity [Statement;0]: For every real $x$, $\exp(x)>0$ and $$\exp(-x)=\frac1{\exp(x)}.$$
L3 -> thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing [Statement;1]: The exponential function is continuous and strictly increasing on $\mathbb R$.
L3 -> thm-exponential-addition-formula [Statement;0]: For all real $x,y$, $$\exp(x+y)=\exp(x)\exp(y).$$

## cex-exponential-not-uniformly-continuous-on-r
L1 -> def-uniform-continuity-real [Definition;2]: and uniform continuity is
L2 -> thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing [Statement;3]: The exponential function is continuous and strictly increasing on $\mathbb R$.
L2 -> cor-mean-value-theorem [Statement;0]: Let $a, b \in \mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \to \mathbb{R}$ be continuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and differentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$ ([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \in (a,b)$ with
L2 -> thm-exponential-beats-every-polynomial [Statement;0]: For every $m\in\mathbb N$ and every real $a>0$, $$\frac{x^m}{\exp(ax)}\longrightarrow0\qquad(x\to+\infty).$$
L3 -> cor-archimedean-reciprocal [Statement;1]: This is the **reciprocal form** of the Archimedean property. [[thm-of-archimedean]] on its own delivers only the assertion that the canonical naturals are cofinal, $x < n \cdot 1_F$; the form actually used in analysis, that the reciprocals of the naturals get below every positive bound, is the state

